<% content_for "header" do %><title><%= I18n.translate(locale, "Privacy") %> - Invidious</title><% end %>
<article class="reading-page legal-page" lang="en-US" dir="ltr" aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
<header class="page-heading"><h1 id="privacy-heading">Privacy</h1></header>
<p>This page describes data handled by this version of Invidious. Your account belongs to this instance. Its operator controls the server, configuration, access to stored data, backups and external proxy logs. This page does not promise how long an operator keeps backups or historical logs.</p>
<nav class="reading-index" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#account-data">Account data</a><a href="#browser-data">Browser data</a><a href="#request-data">Requests and verification</a><a href="#external-services">External services</a><a href="#remove-data">Export and removal</a></nav>

<section id="account-data" aria-labelledby="account-data-heading"><h2 id="account-data-heading">Account data stored by this instance</h2>
<p>Registration uses a username and password, without a contact email or email recovery. New accounts have an independent, random owner ID. Legacy owner IDs may differ. Passwords are stored as hashes; they are not stored as readable passwords.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Access:</strong> username, owner ID, password hash and credential version; browser/API sessions with issuance and expiry; a private subscription-feed token; temporary authentication nonces and TV-pairing approval records.</li>
<li><strong>Preferences and subscriptions:</strong> account preferences, channel subscriptions, notification video IDs and feed-refresh state. Preferences can include channel-specific SponsorBlock and title/thumbnail masking rules, playback choices, filters and chat options.</li>
<li><strong>Library:</strong> owned playlists and their ordered video occurrences, saved playlist/mix references, playlist privacy and retained descriptive metadata. Public playlists are visible to others; unlisted playlists are accessible through their link; private playlists require the owner.</li>
<li><strong>Viewing:</strong> watched video IDs, saved titles and channel metadata, release and watch dates, archived watch dates and known durations. History recording and saved playback-position synchronization have separate settings. When enabled, account position storage holds up to 1,000 videos, with entries older than 365 days expiring.</li>
<li><strong>Other account features:</strong> manual channel blocks, per-video chat timing offsets and owned clips. Native clips store a title, public creator attribution, source metadata and an immutable time range; they do not store a copy of the video. Anyone with a clip link can view it while its source remains available.</li>
<li><strong>Optional DeArrow contributions:</strong> a private contribution identity is stored encrypted using a separate server key. An operator with both the database and that key can decrypt it. Ordinary preferences and portable exports do not contain this private identity.</li>
</ul>
<p>Video/channel metadata, avatars, original titles, community lists and other shared caches can also exist independently of an account. Display masking or blocking changes what supported screens show; it does not delete source metadata from shared caches.</p></section>

<section id="browser-data" aria-labelledby="browser-data-heading"><h2 id="browser-data-heading">Data stored in your browser</h2>
<p>Cookies keep you signed in and bind security forms. Browser-profile markers select locally scoped state; they are not account credentials. When instance verification is enabled, a separate verification cookie can grant temporary content access without signing you in.</p>
<p>Cookies, local storage and session storage can hold guest preferences, playback positions and player choices, sidebar and theater/docking choices, subscription views and notification ownership, enhancement caches and browsing restoration state. Supported ordinary GET pages can retain route/continuation and scroll descriptors for Back navigation. Private POST searches are not saved for query restoration. Browser-only state is not a cross-device backup and can be unavailable when storage is denied or full.</p>
<p>Guest and account profiles are isolated. Signing in does not import guest preferences into the account. <strong>Logging out changes the active profile but preserves locally stored profiles.</strong> To remove site data from this device, use your browser's site-data controls for cookies, local storage and session storage. Account deletion asks the next document to purge that account's scoped browser state; it does not clear other profiles or other devices.</p></section>

<section id="request-data" aria-labelledby="request-data-heading"><h2 id="request-data-heading">Requests, logs and access verification</h2>
<p>Serving a request exposes its network address and request data to the server and any configured reverse proxy. Authentication throttling derives keyed hashes from usernames and client IP addresses and stores attempt counts and expiry windows in PostgreSQL. The recognized address depends on the operator's trusted-proxy configuration. This is not a permanent account lockout.</p>
<p>The application's standard request log records time, response status, method, a path and elapsed time. It omits query strings and redacts credential-bearing webhook paths. Error or diagnostic logging and any reverse-proxy, hosting or CDN logging are separate; the operator must protect and configure them. Do not put passwords, API tokens or private feed links into reports.</p>
<p>If the operator enables Cloudflare Turnstile, the verification page loads Cloudflare's widget and sends its challenge response to this instance for provider verification. A verification pass is a reusable access credential until expiry or a policy change. Verification is separate from account login. Native clients receive the existing completion event and manage their own credential storage. Cloudflare handles its widget data under its <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noreferrer noopener">privacy policy</a>.</p></section>

<section id="external-services" aria-labelledby="external-services-heading"><h2 id="external-services-heading">External services and public contributions</h2>
<p>The server contacts YouTube and the configured Companion for content and playback information. Depending on proxy and Companion settings, your browser can request media directly from Google or the configured public Companion, exposing its network address to that service. Google describes its handling in its <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noreferrer noopener">privacy policy</a>. Following an external link also sends a request to that destination.</p>
<p>Optional features contact their providers: archived annotations, SponsorBlock/DeArrow data and AiSList community lists. SponsorBlock and DeArrow lookups use a four-character video-hash prefix through the server. AiSList downloads community lists without sending account identifiers or history to the list host.</p>
<p>Submitting a DeArrow title or vote is an explicit upstream contribution using your contribution identity. Contributions can be public and are not removed by deleting an Invidious account or its local identity. Browser notifications require permission; subscribed-upload events are delivered by this instance. Imports are sent to this instance for parsing and can apply in parts; portable exports contain private account data and should be stored carefully.</p></section>

<section id="remove-data" aria-labelledby="remove-data-heading"><h2 id="remove-data-heading">Export, controls and removal</h2>
<p>Use Data and exports for the available JSON account export and subscription OPML formats. These are partial portable formats, not complete backups: they omit features such as clips, session credentials, private DeArrow identity and browser-only state. Importing is not one account-wide transaction.</p>
<p>Account controls let you revoke sessions and change credentials. Revoked credentials stop granting access; this does not erase copies already stored on other devices. Clearing watch history also clears saved account positions; disabling position synchronization removes saved account positions. Disabling history recording preserves existing history until you remove it.</p>
<p>Deleting an account requires its current password and removes its owned data, including owned playlists, sessions, history, saved references, clips, blocks, chat offsets and encrypted contribution identity. TV records can retain their expired/state information without account ownership. Deletion does not remove shared caches, previously accepted upstream contributions or copies in operator-managed backups and historical logs. Backup and log retention depends on the operator.</p>
<div class="toolbar"><a class="button" href="/data_control">Data and exports</a><a class="button" href="/preferences">Settings</a><a class="button" href="/account">Account</a><a class="button" href="/clear_watch_history">Clear watch history</a><a class="button button-danger" href="/delete_account">Delete account</a></div></section>
</article>
